## 1. Objective and scope

Determine why teams using the same nominal workplace process exhibit persistent site-level differences in completion time.

Treat the documented process as a specification and each site’s implementation as a system under test. The study will:

- Verify whether sites execute behaviorally equivalent processes.
- Identify where completion-time differences arise.
- Test competing explanations:
  1. different case/input mix;
  2. inconsistent process implementation;
  3. local resource or capacity constraints;
  4. handoff, dependency, or queue delays;
  5. differences in tools, infrastructure, or software configuration;
  6. staffing, experience, and learning effects;
  7. measurement or timestamp artifacts;
  8. unobserved site context.
- Distinguish differences in productive work time from waiting, rework, and blocked time.
- Estimate which explanations are supported and how much of the between-site difference each explains.

The primary outcome is end-to-end completion time. Secondary outcomes include active processing time, waiting time, handoff time, rework, first-pass success, and tail latency.

## 2. Assumptions

- The process has defined entry and completion conditions that can be translated into testable acceptance criteria.
- A “case” or work item can be tracked across major process states.
- Sites process sufficiently comparable work, or differences can be measured and adjusted for.
- Historical timestamps are not assumed valid until tested against independent evidence.
- Site differences may be caused by multiple interacting factors rather than one root cause.
- Staff behavior may change under observation; passive data collection should therefore be preferred where possible.
- The investigation concerns system and process performance, not individual performance appraisal.
- Access controls, privacy protections, and data minimization will be established before collection.
- Any intervention used for causal identification must be operationally safe, ethically acceptable, and preapproved.

## 3. Research design

### 3.1 Convert the nominal process into an executable specification

Create a process specification containing:

- permitted states and state transitions;
- required inputs and outputs;
- entry and exit criteria;
- role and approval requirements;
- expected tool use;
- exception and escalation paths;
- service-level expectations;
- definitions of completion, failure, cancellation, and rework.

Resolve ambiguities with process owners before comparing sites. Preserve unresolved ambiguity as a finding rather than forcing artificial agreement.

### 3.2 Select sites and observation periods

Use all eligible sites where feasible. Otherwise, purposively sample sites representing:

- consistently fast, median, and slow completion times;
- different volumes, staffing levels, time zones, and organizational contexts;
- different tool or infrastructure configurations.

Observe enough time to cover normal demand variability, staffing cycles, month-end or seasonal effects, and learning trends. Define the study period prospectively and conduct a historical baseline analysis where data quality permits.

### 3.3 Establish comparability and repeatability

Develop a shared test suite of de-identified reference cases spanning:

- common low-, medium-, and high-complexity cases;
- boundary conditions;
- incomplete or erroneous inputs;
- cases requiring handoffs or approvals;
- exception and recovery paths.

Before using these cases operationally, validate that they are realistic and do not create safety, compliance, or customer risk. Where live reference cases are inappropriate, use a sandbox, simulation, tabletop trace, or retrospective replay.

Repeat selected cases within sites, across sites, and at different times. Randomize case order when practical to reduce sequence and learning effects.

### 3.4 Observational phase

Instrument actual cases to capture detailed event logs. For each case, reconstruct the full execution trace from entry to completion.

Conduct process-conformance testing:

- compare observed paths with the specification;
- identify skipped, repeated, reordered, or locally added steps;
- record exception handling and manual workarounds;
- compare tool versions, settings, permissions, templates, and integrations;
- identify where cases wait, queue, fail, or are returned.

Use structured observations and interviews only to explain trace patterns, not as the sole evidence for timing or compliance.

### 3.5 Pre-specified competing explanations and discriminating tests

| Explanation | Predicted observation | Observation that weakens it |
|---|---|---|
| Case mix | Site effect declines after matching or adjustment for pre-entry complexity | Similar reference cases remain substantially slower |
| Process drift | Slow sites use extra, reordered, repeated, or nonstandard steps | Execution traces are equivalent across sites |
| Capacity or congestion | Delay increases with queue length, utilization, absence, or demand spikes | Differences persist during low-load periods and after load adjustment |
| Handoffs/dependencies | Excess time clusters between roles or external responses | Most differences occur within active processing steps |
| Tools/infrastructure | Latency aligns with tool version, configuration, outages, or response time | Standardized-tool tests produce the same site ranking |
| Experience/learning | Faster completion associates with relevant tenure or case familiarity | Differences persist among similarly experienced staff and over time |
| Rework/quality tradeoff | Faster sites have lower first-pass quality or more downstream defects | Quality is equivalent or better at faster sites |
| Measurement artifact | Alternative clocks or audited traces alter the site ranking | Independent timing sources reproduce the ranking |
| Local policy/context | Differences align with approval rules, schedules, or compliance obligations | Sites with equivalent local constraints still differ |
| Unobserved site effect | Residual site variance remains after measured factors | Site variance becomes negligible after adjustment |

### 3.6 Controlled validation, where feasible

After the observational phase, use low-risk, reversible tests to distinguish explanations:

- process equivalent reference cases at multiple sites;
- temporarily standardize a template, configuration, or routing rule;
- compare matched cases at low- and high-load times;
- randomize eligible cases across teams or shared queues;
- use crossover designs in which sites alternate between existing and standardized configurations.

Specify interventions, outcomes, exclusion criteria, and analysis before examining results. Do not withhold required resources or deliberately create harmful delays.

### 3.7 Quality assurance

- Version-control the process specification, test cases, data dictionary, and analysis code.
- Pilot instrumentation at multiple sites.
- Test event completeness, timestamp order, clock synchronization, duplicate records, and identifier linkage.
- Blind trace reviewers to site identity where practical.
- Double-code a sample of process traces and qualitative observations.
- Maintain a deviation log for protocol changes.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

For each case:

- pseudonymous case and site identifiers;
- process entry and completion timestamps;
- timestamps for every material state transition;
- queue entry/exit, handoff, pause, escalation, return, and rework events;
- cancellation, abandonment, and censoring status;
- case type, complexity, priority, completeness, and exception indicators known at entry;
- actor role, relevant experience category, shift, and workload at assignment;
- tool, version, configuration, integration, and template used;
- quality outcomes, defects, downstream corrections, and first-pass success;
- external dependency requests and response times.

For each site and time interval:

- staffing and scheduled coverage;
- arrivals, backlog, work in progress, queue lengths, and throughput;
- estimated utilization and concurrency;
- operating hours, holidays, outages, and policy changes;
- local approvals, compliance requirements, and escalation rules;
- infrastructure performance and incident logs.

Additional evidence:

- current and historical process documents;
- configuration exports or screen-level verification;
- structured field observations;
- interviews focused on observed anomalies;
- independently timed samples to validate system logs;
- reference-case outputs and trace results.

Collect potential confounders measured before process entry whenever possible. Do not adjust primary models for variables that are consequences of site assignment unless conducting an explicitly defined mediation analysis.

## 5. Measurement

### Primary outcome

Elapsed completion time from a common, operationally observable entry event to a common completion event.

Report:

- median;
- geometric mean where appropriate;
- 75th, 90th, and 95th percentiles;
- probability of completion within defined thresholds;
- survival curves for incomplete cases.

### Time decomposition

Partition end-to-end time into mutually defined components:

- active processing;
- queue or waiting;
- handoff;
- external dependency;
- blocked or paused;
- rework;
- unclassified time.

### Explanatory measures

- process-conformance score;
- number and type of deviations;
- step count and repeated-step count;
- handoff count;
- backlog and utilization at arrival;
- staffing coverage;
- tool response time and failure rate;
- configuration differences;
- case complexity and input completeness;
- relevant experience and recent case volume.

### Quality and balancing measures

- first-pass success;
- defect and reopening rates;
- downstream correction effort;
- customer or stakeholder outcome, if applicable;
- error severity;
- staff burden.

### Measurement validation

- Compare timestamps across at least two sources for an audit sample.
- Quantify missing events, impossible sequences, clock drift, and source disagreement.
- Measure inter-rater agreement for manually classified traces.
- Test whether the definition of entry or completion differs by site.
- Predefine handling of overnight closure, weekends, batching, pauses, censored cases, and outliers.
- Preserve raw elapsed time; derive business-hours time only as a secondary measure.

## 6. Analysis

### 6.1 Descriptive and trace analysis

- Plot case-level timelines and distributions by site.
- Compare arrival rates, backlog, throughput, and quality.
- Use process mining or equivalent trace comparison to locate deviations and bottlenecks.
- Decompose each site’s completion time into processing, waiting, handoff, dependency, and rework components.
- Examine whether site rankings are stable across periods and case types.

### 6.2 Statistical modeling

Because cases are clustered within teams, sites, and time periods, fit hierarchical models with appropriate random effects.

For completion time:

- use accelerated failure-time or Cox-type survival models when censoring exists;
- use log-time mixed models or quantile models for completed cases as complementary analyses;
- allow nonlinear relationships for workload and utilization;
- include site-by-case-type interactions where justified.

Use a staged model sequence:

1. site and calendar effects only;
2. add pre-entry case mix;
3. add demand, capacity, and staffing;
4. add process-path and handoff measures;
5. add tools and configuration;
6. add rework and quality measures for mediation or tradeoff analysis.

Track how estimated site contrasts and residual site variance change at each stage. Treat attenuation as explanatory evidence, not automatic proof of causality.

### 6.3 Causal contrasts

For randomized or crossover tests, estimate intention-to-treat effects with site and period controls. For nonrandom standardized tests, use matched comparisons and explicitly state the remaining identifying assumptions.

Avoid mechanically controlling for mediators when estimating total effects. Conduct separate analyses for:

- total site-associated difference;
- associations attributable to initial case mix;
- mediated contributions from waiting, handoffs, rework, or tooling.

### 6.4 Robustness checks

- Alternative entry and completion definitions.
- Calendar versus business time.
- Exclusion and inclusion of exceptional cases.
- Complete-case versus multiple-imputation analyses.
- Stratification by complexity, volume, and period.
- Fixed-site versus random-site specifications.
- Negative-control outcomes or periods where plausible.
- Placebo checks for pre-entry differences.
- Sensitivity to unmeasured confounding.
- Bootstrap uncertainty for decomposition estimates.
- Replication in a later holdout period.

Report effect sizes and uncertainty intervals, not only significance tests. Correct or hierarchically control multiplicity for numerous site and subgroup comparisons.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Define a practically important difference in advance, both in time units and as a percentage of baseline completion time.

Classify an explanation as **supported** when:

- its predicted trace or contextual pattern is observed;
- its association has a practically meaningful magnitude with an uncertainty interval excluding negligible effects;
- it reduces relevant site contrasts or residual site variance;
- results are robust to pre-specified alternative models;
- and, where feasible, a standardized or controlled test reproduces the predicted change without degrading quality.

Classify it as **partially supported** when evidence is consistent but imprecise, restricted to certain case types, or primarily observational.

Classify it as **not supported** when the predicted pattern is absent, the estimated effect is practically negligible, or discriminating tests contradict it. Absence of evidence will not be labeled evidence of absence when power or measurement quality is inadequate.

Stop data collection when all of the following hold:

- the minimum pre-specified observation period and case counts are met;
- each major case stratum and site has adequate representation;
- timestamp and trace-quality thresholds are satisfied;
- uncertainty intervals for the principal site contrasts and leading explanations are narrower than pre-specified decision margins;
- site rankings and major decomposition results are stable in sequential holdout periods;
- no unresolved high-severity measurement defect remains.

Permit early termination only for privacy, safety, operational harm, or irreparable data-integrity problems. Do not stop solely because a desired explanation reaches statistical significance.

## 8. Limitations

- The nominal process may be underspecified, so “nonconformance” may reflect documentation failure rather than team failure.
- Reference cases may not reproduce the uncertainty, interruptions, or incentives of real work.
- Observational adjustment cannot eliminate unmeasured confounding.
- Staffing, workload, tool configuration, and local policy may change together, limiting attribution.
- Detailed instrumentation may alter behavior.
- Event logs may omit informal communication, cognitive work, or work performed outside official systems.
- Completion speed may conflict with quality, equity, compliance, employee workload, or downstream outcomes.
- Results from sampled sites, periods, and case types may not generalize to other contexts.
- Decomposition methods can depend on model order and assumptions; contributions should not be interpreted as uniquely determined causal shares.
- Site-level findings should not be used to infer individual performance without a separate, appropriately designed study.

